    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get that. but what exactly is the ldl doing to the arteries? like, how does it actually cause the buildup? is it just sitting there or is it actively doing something to the walls??",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL isn't just passively sitting there; it actively contributes to damage. When high levels of LDL penetrate the artery wall, they become oxidized, which triggers an inflammatory response in the lining of the artery. This inflammatory process recruits other substances that form the fatty plaque, which then narrows and hardens the arteries, restricting blood flow.",
      "source": "target",
